      <section class="journal-reading-route" aria-labelledby="shivaratri-telling"><p class="eyebrow">A TELLING FROM THE SUPPLIED BOOKS</p><h4 id="shivaratri-telling">An unexpected act of worship</h4><p>In the Skanda Purana’s Kedara-khanda, a forester named Canda spends a night awake in a bilva tree. Leaves and water fall onto a Shiva linga below, becoming an offering he has not consciously intended. Later, when a dog eats the food brought for him, his response changes from anger toward acceptance. The account connects Shivaratri with transformation and Shiva’s grace.</p><p>This is one textual telling, distinct from the festival’s other sacred associations.</p><a href="/sources.html#supplied-journal-books">Source: Kedara-khanda 33.9–15, 29–45 →</a></section>
